Wrapper Classes

* a class whose object contains or wraps primitive dataType
  (int ,float , char).


* object of a wrapper class contains a field which stores primitive dataType

PDT                 Wrapper class
int                  Integer
float                Float
char                 Character
boolean              Booleans
long                 Long

*-> Why we need this wrapper class
Java is object oriented programming .
When we declare ->  int in = 4;

this is  not a object , it is a normal variable  so this Violate the
oops principle.


java.util package's methods or classes work on object not primitive dataType

java collection framework we have some data-Structure like Arraylist ,
stack , queue they also store object not primitive datatype ,
so wraps the primitive data type into object and pass.


* Arraylist

ARRAYLIST — WHAT YOU ACTUALLY NEED TO KNOW
1. What it is
ArrayList<E> is a resizable array in Java.
Not fixed length like normal arrays
Keeps elements contiguous in memory (good for cache, good for iteration)
Allows random access in O(1) time

2. Internal storage
Backed by a dynamic array:
Object[] elementData;


If capacity runs out → grows automatically.
3. Time Complexity (Don’t skip this)

Operations:

Operation	Time
get(i)	O(1)
set(i)	O(1)
add() at end	Amortized O(1)
add(i, x) at index	O(n) (shift right)
remove(i)	O(n) (shift left)
contains(x)	O(n)
iteration	O(n)

If this table isn’t in your head, you’ll fail algorithm questions that ask “which data structure is optimal?”.

4. Capacity Growth (Critical Detail)
Default initial capacity = 10
newCapacity = oldCapacity + (oldCapacity >> 1)  // approx 1.5x


This prevents stupid growth strategies like doubling or tiny increments.

5. Autoboxing
Since primitives aren’t allowed, Java autoboxes:

ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add(5); // auto: Integer.valueOf(5)


This introduces overhead — don’t pretend it's free.